1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,967
INFO 7375, Computational Skepticism for AI. Lectures. Bias: Where It Enters and Who

2
00:00:04,987 --> 00:00:10,720
Is Responsible. Learn where bias enters an AI system, which fix has the leverage to

3
00:00:10,740 --> 00:00:13,022
move it, and who owns it.

4
00:00:13,842 --> 00:00:18,734
Namaste. This is Liam, in for Bear, with lecture six of INFO 7375, Computational

5
00:00:18,754 --> 00:00:23,296
Skepticism for AI, at Northeastern University. When a system is biased, the reflex

6
00:00:23,316 --> 00:00:29,261
is to fix the model. That's the fix nearest your hands, and very often it isn't the

7
00:00:29,281 --> 00:00:30,313
one that works.

8
00:00:30,333 --> 00:00:35,172
Six terms. An estimator is a rule that turns data into a number. A base rate is how

9
00:00:35,192 --> 00:00:40,031
common an outcome is in a group. A label is the answer a model learns to predict. A

10
00:00:40,051 --> 00:00:44,350
proxy is a feature that stands in for something else. A protected group is a group

11
00:00:44,370 --> 00:00:48,398
shielded from unfair treatment. And a causal graph is a set of arrows showing what

12
00:00:48,418 --> 00:00:48,938
causes what.

13
00:00:48,958 --> 00:00:51,230
Three Teams, One Problem.

14
00:00:51,250 --> 00:00:56,938
Here's a parable, built to isolate a real pattern, not a case from any filing. A

15
00:00:56,958 --> 00:01:00,506
deployed AI system produces biased outcomes, reproducibly. Three engineering teams

16
00:01:00,526 --> 00:01:02,647
get the same job: fix it.

17
00:01:02,667 --> 00:01:07,330
Team one rewrites the loss function. They add a fairness penalty, a term that

18
00:01:07,350 --> 00:01:11,680
punishes unequal error rates across protected groups, and retrain on the same data.

19
00:01:11,700 --> 00:01:14,022
The gap shrinks a little. Not enough.

20
00:01:14,042 --> 00:01:19,084
Team two fixes the data. They find the under-represented group, run a costly new

21
00:01:19,104 --> 00:01:23,785
collection, and retrain. The gap changes shape: smaller in some places, larger in

22
00:01:23,805 --> 00:01:24,147
others.

23
00:01:24,167 --> 00:01:29,063
Team three touches neither the model nor the data. They look at the room the model

24
00:01:29,083 --> 00:01:33,058
was deployed into. A human reviewer reads its outputs, and the reviewer's own

25
00:01:33,078 --> 00:01:37,053
patterns amplified the bias. Team three changes the review process, and the gap

26
00:01:37,073 --> 00:01:38,897
falls by an order of magnitude.

27
00:01:38,917 --> 00:01:42,951
Teams one and two weren't wrong. They made real, small reductions, on real paths

28
00:01:42,971 --> 00:01:47,295
that carried little of the bias. Team three had leverage, and it wasn't luck. It

29
00:01:47,315 --> 00:01:50,480
came from a way of looking the other teams hadn't used.

30
00:01:50,500 --> 00:01:54,931
That way of looking is the subject. The temptation is to fix what's nearest your

31
00:01:54,951 --> 00:01:58,789
hands. The leverage is usually somewhere else. So before fixing anything, ask: where

32
00:01:58,809 --> 00:02:02,647
does this bias live, and which intervention has the most leverage on it?

33
00:02:02,667 --> 00:02:04,480
What Bias Means.

34
00:02:04,500 --> 00:02:08,655
In the newspapers, bias means unfair or prejudiced. That meaning is built for moral

35
00:02:08,675 --> 00:02:12,831
clarity, not for engineering. In statistics, bias is a property of an estimator, a

36
00:02:12,851 --> 00:02:17,603
rule that turns data into a number. The formal meaning gives up the moral punch and

37
00:02:17,623 --> 00:02:21,480
buys you something you can act on: it tells you where to intervene.

38
00:02:21,500 --> 00:02:26,686
Call the true quantity theta, and your estimate theta hat. Bias is the expected

39
00:02:26,706 --> 00:02:31,892
value of theta hat, minus theta. Zero means unbiased: maybe wrong on any one

40
00:02:31,913 --> 00:02:35,983
dataset, but not systematically wrong. Nonzero means systematically off, in one

41
00:02:36,003 --> 00:02:36,355
direction.

42
00:02:36,375 --> 00:02:41,198
Here's the consequence that matters most. More data will not fix a biased estimator.

43
00:02:41,218 --> 00:02:45,696
It converges, with growing confidence, to the wrong answer. More data narrows the

44
00:02:45,716 --> 00:02:47,772
scatter. It doesn't move the offset.

45
00:02:47,792 --> 00:02:52,764
Here is the run behind that picture. The true rate is thirty percent, but the filter

46
00:02:52,784 --> 00:02:57,133
keeps four in ten positives and eight in ten negatives. Whatever the sample size,

47
00:02:57,153 --> 00:03:01,189
the estimate settles near seventeen and a half percent. The spread shrinks. The

48
00:03:01,209 --> 00:03:01,813
error stays.

49
00:03:01,833 --> 00:03:06,285
This is exactly what happened in nineteen thirty-six. The Literary Digest magazine

50
00:03:06,305 --> 00:03:11,129
polled about two point four million people and predicted that Landon would beat

51
00:03:11,149 --> 00:03:14,855
Roosevelt. Roosevelt won with about sixty-one percent of the vote.

52
00:03:14,875 --> 00:03:17,808
Peverill Squire's nineteen eighty-eight analysis found two problems working

53
00:03:17,828 --> 00:03:22,074
together. The mailing list leaned toward people with cars and telephones in the

54
00:03:22,094 --> 00:03:26,667
depths of the Depression, and the people who answered differed again from those who

55
00:03:26,687 --> 00:03:30,605
didn't. More ballots drawn the same way would have repeated the error.

56
00:03:30,625 --> 00:03:35,873
So now we can say what the ten mechanisms of bias are: ten distinct ways to make

57
00:03:35,893 --> 00:03:39,902
that expression, the expected estimate minus the truth, nonzero. Each has its own

58
00:03:39,922 --> 00:03:44,240
causal structure and its own fix. Confuse them and you apply the right intervention

59
00:03:44,260 --> 00:03:45,480
at the wrong place.

60
00:03:45,500 --> 00:03:46,980
Selection Bias.

61
00:03:47,000 --> 00:03:52,790
Here are the ten, as places in a pipeline where the estimate can drift from the

62
00:03:52,810 --> 00:03:57,147
truth. Selection and sampling, when people are collected. Data entry, when they're

63
00:03:57,167 --> 00:04:00,415
recorded. Observer, self-report and coding, when they're measured. Confirmation,

64
00:04:00,435 --> 00:04:02,230
publication, historical and implicit follow.

65
00:04:02,250 --> 00:04:06,444
A note on counting. Later we'll meet pairs, like aggregation with subgroup masking.

66
00:04:06,464 --> 00:04:10,335
Those aren't an eleventh and twelfth mechanism. They're derived interactions of the

67
00:04:10,355 --> 00:04:15,522
ten. The ten are the alphabet, and the pairs are a short list of common words.

68
00:04:15,542 --> 00:04:20,488
Start with selection bias, and watch how it enters. You write a reasonable query:

69
00:04:20,508 --> 00:04:25,454
patients with a complete lab panel within six hours of admission. Nobody decides to

70
00:04:25,474 --> 00:04:28,647
exclude anyone. The exclusion rides in on the reasonableness.

71
00:04:28,667 --> 00:04:32,571
Your estimator computes the average outcome among the people in your sample. You

72
00:04:32,591 --> 00:04:36,798
wanted the average in the population. Those two are equal on exactly one condition:

73
00:04:36,818 --> 00:04:41,628
whether you land in the sample must not depend on the outcome. The moment it does,

74
00:04:41,648 --> 00:04:44,647
the estimate is biased before a single parameter is fit.

75
00:04:44,667 --> 00:04:49,039
Take a sepsis model, as an illustration. The patients too critical to have labs

76
00:04:49,059 --> 00:04:54,058
drawn on time are exactly the ones who most need the alert, and the six-hour filter

77
00:04:54,078 --> 00:04:58,451
drops them. The model learns what predicts sepsis among the stable, then is asked

78
00:04:58,471 --> 00:05:00,647
about the unstable. It underestimates their risk.

79
00:05:00,667 --> 00:05:04,572
You can correct for selection you observed. Inverse probability weighting gives each

80
00:05:04,592 --> 00:05:09,806
kept person a weight of one over their chance of being kept, so survivors stand in

81
00:05:09,826 --> 00:05:14,386
for the people dropped. In the same simulation, the naive estimate is about eighteen

82
00:05:14,406 --> 00:05:16,022
percent. Weighted, it's about thirty.

83
00:05:16,042 --> 00:05:20,312
But weighting only works if the variable that drove the selection was recorded. If

84
00:05:20,332 --> 00:05:24,910
it wasn't, you have nothing to weight on. That isn't a modeling problem you can

85
00:05:24,930 --> 00:05:29,200
solve at your desk. It's a data-collection problem, and the honest answer is to

86
00:05:29,220 --> 00:05:29,813
collect differently.

87
00:05:29,833 --> 00:05:31,480
Judgment in the Loop.

88
00:05:31,500 --> 00:05:36,240
Five more mechanisms share a spine: somewhere a person exercises judgment, and their

89
00:05:36,260 --> 00:05:41,000
prior leaks into the record. Confirmation bias is a researcher's prior shaping what

90
00:05:41,020 --> 00:05:45,393
gets collected, kept and reported. Observer bias is that prior inside one

91
00:05:45,413 --> 00:05:48,688
measurement, like an annotator who knows the earlier diagnosis.

92
00:05:48,708 --> 00:05:53,624
Self-report bias is the subject's own prior: people report voting more and drinking

93
00:05:53,644 --> 00:05:58,179
less than they do. Data-coding bias enters when judgment squeezes material into

94
00:05:58,199 --> 00:06:02,735
categories. And publication bias is the same leak across a whole literature:

95
00:06:02,755 --> 00:06:05,772
significant results get printed, nulls stay in drawers.

96
00:06:05,792 --> 00:06:10,356
The diagnostic where two humans code the same thing is Cohen's kappa: agreement

97
00:06:10,376 --> 00:06:14,588
beyond chance. If kappa collapses when you unblind the annotators, that's direct

98
00:06:14,608 --> 00:06:19,525
evidence of observer bias. In a constructed example, it falls from point six to

99
00:06:19,545 --> 00:06:20,230
point two.

100
00:06:20,250 --> 00:06:24,659
Low kappa on particular items rather than particular people points at data-coding

101
00:06:24,679 --> 00:06:29,087
instead: the category is underspecified, not the coder. For publication bias the

102
00:06:29,107 --> 00:06:34,623
analogue is the funnel plot. A missing lower-left corner is the drawer of small null

103
00:06:34,643 --> 00:06:35,730
studies made visible.

104
00:06:35,750 --> 00:06:40,600
None of these five entered through the model. They entered upstream of the data,

105
00:06:40,620 --> 00:06:44,426
when a human judged. So the fixes look like organizational practice:

106
00:06:44,446 --> 00:06:47,556
pre-registration, blinding, adversarial review. Engineers reach past them because

107
00:06:47,576 --> 00:06:51,730
they don't look like engineering, which is exactly why the bias survives.

108
00:06:51,750 --> 00:06:56,351
Two more are quieter, and people mistake them for noise. Data-entry bias is

109
00:06:56,371 --> 00:07:00,616
patterned transcription error: a form that buries the accurate diagnosis code three

110
00:07:00,636 --> 00:07:05,592
menus deep gets the nearest code instead, and a whole class of conditions is

111
00:07:05,612 --> 00:07:09,147
undercoded in one direction. Verify against source documents; don't auto-correct.

112
00:07:09,167 --> 00:07:12,853
Sampling bias is selection's accessibility cousin: some groups are under-represented

113
00:07:12,873 --> 00:07:18,042
because they were hard to reach, not because of their outcomes. A remote-work survey

114
00:07:18,062 --> 00:07:21,008
pushed through LinkedIn over-represents credentialed professionals. When outside

115
00:07:21,028 --> 00:07:26,568
data gives the true sizes, weight each group by its population share over its sample

116
00:07:26,588 --> 00:07:26,938
share.

117
00:07:26,958 --> 00:07:29,522
Historical and Implicit Bias.

118
00:07:29,542 --> 00:07:33,988
Now the one that breaks the pattern. In every mechanism so far, something was wrong

119
00:07:34,008 --> 00:07:38,751
with the data: a filter, a prior, a slip. Historical bias has none of that. The

120
00:07:38,771 --> 00:07:42,622
sample is representative. The labels are accurate. The data is a faithful recording

121
00:07:42,642 --> 00:07:45,897
of the world that produced it. And it is still poison.

122
00:07:45,917 --> 00:07:50,920
Imagine a hiring model trained on ten years of promotion records at a company that

123
00:07:50,940 --> 00:07:54,939
promoted men into engineering roles at higher rates. It learns the historical

124
00:07:54,959 --> 00:07:59,628
outcome given the candidate, and learns it well. Every metric comes back green. But

125
00:07:59,648 --> 00:08:03,647
the historical outcome isn't the fair outcome. Its accuracy is the harm.

126
00:08:03,667 --> 00:08:08,414
So the fixes can't be data-quality fixes; there's no defect to repair. They lower

127
00:08:08,434 --> 00:08:12,841
the leverage of the historical signal on today's prediction: a less-biased proxy for

128
00:08:12,861 --> 00:08:16,927
the outcome, a fairness constraint, or weighting recent records over records from

129
00:08:16,947 --> 00:08:21,013
more discriminatory periods. Each trades predictive fit for less inheritance, and a

130
00:08:21,033 --> 00:08:23,397
person decides how much fit to spend.

131
00:08:23,417 --> 00:08:27,439
Implicit bias, the unconscious associations of the people in the pipeline, isn't

132
00:08:27,459 --> 00:08:32,829
really a tenth peer. It isn't an entry point. It's the medium several of them swim

133
00:08:32,849 --> 00:08:36,535
in: an underground water table, with confirmation, observer, coding and publication

134
00:08:36,555 --> 00:08:39,230
bias as the springs where it reaches daylight.

135
00:08:39,250 --> 00:08:44,376
Because it has no single point, it resists a point fix. One handle is a downstream

136
00:08:44,396 --> 00:08:49,201
audit: compare the rate of positive predictions across groups. But read a gap as a

137
00:08:49,221 --> 00:08:54,025
flag, not a verdict, since real differences in base rates produce the same gap. The

138
00:08:54,045 --> 00:08:57,242
fixes with leverage are structural: diverse teams, structured decisions, continuous

139
00:08:57,262 --> 00:08:57,563
auditing.

140
00:08:57,583 --> 00:09:00,438
Three Flavors, and How They Compound.

141
00:09:00,458 --> 00:09:04,926
When people say the model is biased, they can mean three different things. Dataset

142
00:09:04,946 --> 00:09:09,415
bias: the training set doesn't represent the people the system is deployed on. A

143
00:09:09,435 --> 00:09:13,582
face system trained mostly on light-skinned faces, then used on everyone, is the

144
00:09:13,602 --> 00:09:16,147
textbook example. The bias is in the sample.

145
00:09:16,167 --> 00:09:20,678
Label bias: the labels encode a biased process. A recidivism predictor trained on

146
00:09:20,698 --> 00:09:24,513
arrest data isn't predicting who commits crimes. It's predicting who gets

147
00:09:24,533 --> 00:09:28,695
re-arrested. Those are different variables, and re-arrest is a biased measurement of

148
00:09:28,715 --> 00:09:30,438
the thing we care about.

149
00:09:30,458 --> 00:09:35,683
Structural bias is the hard case. The data is fine, the labels are fine, the model

150
00:09:35,703 --> 00:09:40,272
does its job, and the outcomes are still unfair. A hiring model perfectly predicts

151
00:09:40,292 --> 00:09:44,533
who succeeds at a company today, but success depends on mentorship, sponsorship and

152
00:09:44,553 --> 00:09:48,466
interesting projects that were never evenly distributed. The model is correct, and

153
00:09:48,486 --> 00:09:50,105
its correctness is the problem.

154
00:09:50,125 --> 00:09:55,304
Put them side by side. Dataset bias lives in the sample, and the fix is to resample

155
00:09:55,324 --> 00:09:59,585
or broaden collection. Label bias lives in the labeling step: fix the process or

156
00:09:59,605 --> 00:10:04,478
find a better proxy. Structural bias lives in the world the model sits in, and no

157
00:10:04,498 --> 00:10:06,313
model or data fix reaches it.

158
00:10:06,333 --> 00:10:11,174
Treat structural bias as a dataset problem and you'll spend a quarter and move the

159
00:10:11,194 --> 00:10:15,711
disparity nowhere. Bear calls that the most common failure in this field. That's his

160
00:10:15,731 --> 00:10:17,980
judgment from experience, not a measured rate.

161
00:10:18,000 --> 00:10:22,784
In real systems several mechanisms run at once. Selection compounds historical bias:

162
00:10:22,804 --> 00:10:27,989
there's less data on the under-represented group, and the data that exists was

163
00:10:28,009 --> 00:10:31,993
generated under discriminatory conditions. Rebalancing fixes the first, and makes

164
00:10:32,013 --> 00:10:36,397
the second worse. You now hold more discriminatory data, accurately represented.

165
00:10:36,417 --> 00:10:40,831
Observer bias compounds with data coding. Annotator priors become categorical rules

166
00:10:40,851 --> 00:10:45,669
baked into the labels. Agreement scores capture the combined effect but can't

167
00:10:45,689 --> 00:10:50,506
separate the two. Disagreement clustered on items points to coding. Clustered on

168
00:10:50,526 --> 00:10:52,522
annotators, it points to observers.

169
00:10:52,542 --> 00:10:57,206
Confirmation compounds with publication. A team designs a study to confirm its

170
00:10:57,226 --> 00:11:01,890
hypothesis, gets a positive result, and publishes, while null results from other

171
00:11:01,910 --> 00:11:06,574
groups vanish. Trim-and-fill can correct the publication part. It cannot touch the

172
00:11:06,594 --> 00:11:07,355
study design.

173
00:11:07,375 --> 00:11:12,890
Bear keeps a short working list of pairs that amplify each other: selection with

174
00:11:12,910 --> 00:11:16,449
historical, observer with coding, confirmation with publication, sampling with

175
00:11:16,469 --> 00:11:19,612
measurement, aggregation with subgroup masking, evaluation with deployment,

176
00:11:19,632 --> 00:11:23,566
annotation with class imbalance, and linkage with re-identification. Once you

177
00:11:23,586 --> 00:11:25,938
confirm one, check its partner first.

178
00:11:25,958 --> 00:11:28,438
A Dataset Is Not the World.

179
00:11:28,458 --> 00:11:33,525
A dataset isn't the world. It's a recording of a slice of it, made by particular

180
00:11:33,545 --> 00:11:37,022
people, at a particular time, with particular instruments, for particular purposes.

181
00:11:37,042 --> 00:11:41,472
Each leaves a fingerprint. To use a dataset responsibly, read it as a historian

182
00:11:41,492 --> 00:11:45,605
reads an archive: what was collected, what was excluded, who decided, and why.

183
00:11:45,625 --> 00:11:50,038
Take COMPAS, a risk-assessment tool used in Broward County, Florida. In twenty

184
00:11:50,058 --> 00:11:54,470
sixteen, ProPublica analyzed scores for people arrested there in twenty thirteen and

185
00:11:54,490 --> 00:11:58,534
twenty fourteen. Among defendants who did not re-offend, forty-four point nine

186
00:11:58,554 --> 00:12:02,966
percent of Black defendants had been labeled higher risk, against twenty-three point

187
00:12:02,986 --> 00:12:04,813
five percent of white defendants.

188
00:12:04,833 --> 00:12:09,713
And among defendants who did re-offend, forty-seven point seven percent of white

189
00:12:09,733 --> 00:12:14,205
defendants had been labeled lower risk, against twenty-eight percent of Black

190
00:12:14,225 --> 00:12:18,697
defendants. As ProPublica reported them, the errors fell unevenly, in opposite

191
00:12:18,717 --> 00:12:19,105
directions.

192
00:12:19,125 --> 00:12:23,375
The tool's maker, Northpointe, answered with a different analysis. Within each risk

193
00:12:23,395 --> 00:12:28,357
score, they said, about the same share of people re-offended in both groups. In

194
00:12:28,377 --> 00:12:32,272
their terms, the score was equally accurate for everyone: predictive parity.

195
00:12:32,292 --> 00:12:36,649
There's something deeper in the data. The label wasn't whether this person committed

196
00:12:36,669 --> 00:12:40,690
another crime. It was whether this person was re-arrested. Re-arrest depends on

197
00:12:40,710 --> 00:12:45,404
crime and on policing. If policing is uneven, re-arrest is an uneven measurement of

198
00:12:45,424 --> 00:12:48,772
crime, and every model trained on it inherits the unevenness.

199
00:12:48,792 --> 00:12:52,393
The modeler thinks they're predicting recidivism. They're actually predicting

200
00:12:52,413 --> 00:12:56,417
re-arrest, given recidivism, given policing, given everything that shapes both.

201
00:12:56,437 --> 00:13:01,245
Deployed without that frame, the model launders the unevenness through an algorithm,

202
00:13:01,265 --> 00:13:02,855
and makes it invisible.

203
00:13:02,875 --> 00:13:05,522
Two Fairness Claims, Both Correct.

204
00:13:05,542 --> 00:13:10,058
Both analyses were correct. They were measuring different things. When two groups

205
00:13:10,078 --> 00:13:14,595
have different base rates, no score can satisfy both. Kleinberg, Mullainathan and

206
00:13:14,615 --> 00:13:19,509
Raghavan proved it in a twenty sixteen paper, and Chouldechova showed the same

207
00:13:19,529 --> 00:13:21,022
incompatibility for recidivism tools.

208
00:13:21,042 --> 00:13:25,105
Here's the plain version. Suppose group one has a true re-offense rate of thirty

209
00:13:25,125 --> 00:13:29,480
percent, and group two fifty. Make the score calibrated, so a given score means the

210
00:13:29,500 --> 00:13:34,147
same in both. Now to equalize false positives you'd have to put the threshold in a

211
00:13:34,167 --> 00:13:37,647
different place for each group, which is treating people differently by group.

212
00:13:37,667 --> 00:13:41,917
Here's the algebra. For a calibrated score, the odds of being right when flagged

213
00:13:41,937 --> 00:13:46,492
equal the base-rate odds, times the ratio of the true positive rate to the false

214
00:13:46,512 --> 00:13:51,067
positive rate. Hold the first equal, with different base rates, and the ratio of the

215
00:13:51,087 --> 00:13:52,897
rates has to differ between groups.

216
00:13:52,917 --> 00:13:57,699
Run the numbers. Fix the accuracy of a flag at sixty percent and the true positive

217
00:13:57,719 --> 00:14:01,902
rate at seventy percent for both groups. Then the false positive rate is forced:

218
00:14:01,922 --> 00:14:06,404
twenty percent in group one, and forty-six point seven in group two. That gap isn't

219
00:14:06,424 --> 00:14:07,605
a bug. It's arithmetic.

220
00:14:07,625 --> 00:14:11,393
The three criteria are calibration, equal false positive rates, and equal false

221
00:14:11,413 --> 00:14:15,498
negative rates. With different base rates they cannot all hold at once, unless

222
00:14:15,518 --> 00:14:19,286
prediction is perfect. So the choice between fairness metrics isn't a technical

223
00:14:19,306 --> 00:14:24,022
choice. It's a values claim: which kind of error are we less willing to make?

224
00:14:24,042 --> 00:14:29,278
Who bears the cost of each error, what the model is used for, and the politics of

225
00:14:29,298 --> 00:14:33,298
the deployment settle that choice. Engineers who treat it as pure optimization are

226
00:14:33,318 --> 00:14:37,318
making a values choice anyway: the one their loss function encodes, while appearing

227
00:14:37,338 --> 00:14:41,337
neutral. That's Professor Bear's argument. I'm presenting it as an argument, not a

228
00:14:41,357 --> 00:14:41,647
finding.

229
00:14:41,667 --> 00:14:46,855
One case to be careful with. In twenty nineteen the Apple Card's credit limits drew

230
00:14:46,875 --> 00:14:50,674
allegations of bias. New York's financial regulator investigated, and in March

231
00:14:50,694 --> 00:14:54,147
twenty twenty-one reported no evidence of unlawful discrimination: applicants with

232
00:14:54,167 --> 00:14:57,966
similar credit characteristics generally had similar outcomes. It did fault customer

233
00:14:57,986 --> 00:15:03,522
service and transparency. So treat it as a case of how a claim gets adjudicated, not

234
00:15:03,542 --> 00:15:04,563
a proven disparity.

235
00:15:04,583 --> 00:15:08,674
So pick the audit criterion that fits the harm structure of the deployment.

236
00:15:08,694 --> 00:15:12,784
Calibration parity means a score means the same for every group. Equal false

237
00:15:12,804 --> 00:15:16,894
positive rates share the cost of being wrongly flagged. Equal false negative rates

238
00:15:16,914 --> 00:15:20,688
share the cost of being missed. The theorem rules out all three.

239
00:15:20,708 --> 00:15:21,980
Pearl's Ladder.

240
00:15:22,000 --> 00:15:26,938
To tell a system that merely correlates a bias from one that causes it, we need a

241
00:15:26,958 --> 00:15:31,022
tool from Judea Pearl: a ladder of causal reasoning, with three rungs. We'll work

242
00:15:31,042 --> 00:15:31,897
the first two.

243
00:15:31,917 --> 00:15:37,016
Rung one is association: what's the probability of Y, given that we observe X? It's

244
00:15:37,036 --> 00:15:41,452
what most machine learning lives on. It's what calibration curves describe, and what

245
00:15:41,472 --> 00:15:45,547
most fairness metrics measure. It's also where most thinking about deployed AI

246
00:15:45,567 --> 00:15:46,230
quietly stops.

247
00:15:46,250 --> 00:15:51,468
Rung two is intervention: the probability of Y if we set X to a value. Pearl writes

248
00:15:51,488 --> 00:15:56,090
it with do. The notation does real work. Observing X can be tangled up with

249
00:15:56,110 --> 00:15:59,480
everything that moves with X. Making X happen severs those tangles.

250
00:15:59,500 --> 00:16:03,743
The classic illustration is the rooster and the sun. Watch roosters and sunrises,

251
00:16:03,763 --> 00:16:08,989
and the sun is very likely up after a crow. But prevent the rooster from crowing,

252
00:16:09,009 --> 00:16:12,924
and the sun rises anyway. Observation said they were associated. Intervention said

253
00:16:12,944 --> 00:16:14,563
the rooster isn't the cause.

254
00:16:14,583 --> 00:16:18,592
Rung three is counterfactual: what would have happened to this specific case if X

255
00:16:18,612 --> 00:16:22,334
had been different, holding everything else fixed? It's the hardest to reach from

256
00:16:22,354 --> 00:16:26,938
data alone. Here it just sits at the top of the ladder, visible, not yet climbed.

257
00:16:26,958 --> 00:16:31,528
For our problem, the probability of loan denial, given race equals x, is the

258
00:16:31,548 --> 00:16:35,462
observational quantity. It's what the model sees in training data. The probability

259
00:16:35,482 --> 00:16:40,379
of denial if we set race to x, holding everything else fixed, is interventional. It

260
00:16:40,399 --> 00:16:44,313
asks whether the decision would change if we changed only that variable.

261
00:16:44,333 --> 00:16:49,539
The two can come apart. A model can show no disparity on rung one while hiding one

262
00:16:49,559 --> 00:16:54,149
on rung two, if you intervene on race-correlated features. Or it can show a rung-one

263
00:16:54,169 --> 00:16:57,838
disparity that vanishes on rung two, because it runs entirely through legitimate

264
00:16:57,858 --> 00:17:02,141
features. Without telling them apart, you can't tell a fair model from one that's

265
00:17:02,161 --> 00:17:03,063
merely been smoothed.

266
00:17:03,083 --> 00:17:07,007
Most deployed mitigation works on rung one: adjust the conditional distributions

267
00:17:07,027 --> 00:17:11,668
until the metric reads the way the engineer wants. That's sometimes useful and

268
00:17:11,688 --> 00:17:15,970
rarely sufficient, because whether a variable causes the bias is a rung-two

269
00:17:15,990 --> 00:17:18,480
question, and rung one can't answer it.

270
00:17:18,500 --> 00:17:23,487
Here are the same audit questions asked at each rung. For loan denial: is the rate

271
00:17:23,507 --> 00:17:28,182
equal across groups, or would it change if race changed and nothing else did? For

272
00:17:28,202 --> 00:17:32,250
hiring: is the hire rate proportional, or would two identical applicants be treated

273
00:17:32,270 --> 00:17:32,563
differently?

274
00:17:32,583 --> 00:17:34,230
Finding the Leverage.

275
00:17:34,250 --> 00:17:39,133
Back to the three teams. Each intervened at a different point in the chain from the

276
00:17:39,153 --> 00:17:44,036
world to the decision. Team one on the model. Team two on the training data. Team

277
00:17:44,056 --> 00:17:47,713
three on the deployment context. All three were interventions, rung-two doings. But

278
00:17:47,733 --> 00:17:48,938
they had different leverage.

279
00:17:48,958 --> 00:17:53,453
Draw the causal graph. The protected attribute sits at the top. Below it, proxies,

280
00:17:53,473 --> 00:17:57,645
then the features the model uses, then the model's output. Then the deployment

281
00:17:57,665 --> 00:18:01,514
context: the reviewer, the threshold, the appeal process. And finally the outcome

282
00:18:01,534 --> 00:18:03,772
that lands on a real person's life.

283
00:18:03,792 --> 00:18:08,244
Now list every path from the protected attribute to the outcome. In this constructed

284
00:18:08,264 --> 00:18:12,716
graph there are ten. Some run through the model. Some bypass it, through proxies

285
00:18:12,736 --> 00:18:17,188
straight into the reviewer or the threshold. Here is what each fix leaves open.

286
00:18:17,208 --> 00:18:22,054
Team one blocked the paths through the model, and left four of ten open. Team two

287
00:18:22,074 --> 00:18:26,311
reshaped the sample, and left eight open. Team three, at the reviewer and the

288
00:18:26,331 --> 00:18:30,568
threshold, blocked all ten. Counting paths isn't measuring bias, but it shows what a

289
00:18:30,588 --> 00:18:31,480
fix can't reach.

290
00:18:31,500 --> 00:18:36,063
The procedure, in working form. One: draw the causal graph, from protected attribute

291
00:18:36,083 --> 00:18:40,647
through proxies, features, output and decision, to the outcome. Two: list every path

292
00:18:40,667 --> 00:18:45,230
from the attribute to the outcome, including the ones that bypass the model.

293
00:18:45,250 --> 00:18:49,661
Three: for each candidate intervention, ask which paths it blocks and which it

294
00:18:49,681 --> 00:18:54,432
leaves open. Four: pick the one that blocks the largest share of the bias-carrying

295
00:18:54,452 --> 00:18:58,522
paths, ideally without cutting paths the deployment needs for its core function.

296
00:18:58,542 --> 00:19:03,549
Underneath is Descartes' doubt, turned on a pipeline. What would have to be true for

297
00:19:03,569 --> 00:19:07,907
this bias to live here? Suspend the tempting explanation, the model, nearest your

298
00:19:07,927 --> 00:19:11,929
hands, and make each candidate location survive the question. The location that

299
00:19:11,949 --> 00:19:13,605
survives is where you intervene.

300
00:19:13,625 --> 00:19:18,172
Why isn't this done more often? The model and the data are inside the engineer's

301
00:19:18,192 --> 00:19:22,131
house. The reviewer's behavior, the appeals process, the way the score gets read,

302
00:19:22,151 --> 00:19:26,089
live in someone else's. Drawing the full graph means crossing those boundaries, and

303
00:19:26,109 --> 00:19:29,438
the engineer who tries is often told it isn't their job.

304
00:19:29,458 --> 00:19:31,355
Who Owns the Bias.

305
00:19:31,375 --> 00:19:35,095
Professor Bear argues that the political dimension of bias is the technical

306
00:19:35,115 --> 00:19:39,147
dimension. Which intervention works depends on which kind of bias you have. Which

307
00:19:39,167 --> 00:19:43,511
kind you have depends on a structural analysis of the deployment, the part people

308
00:19:43,531 --> 00:19:47,563
call political. Skip it and you produce tweaks that don't move the disparity.

309
00:19:47,583 --> 00:19:52,230
One of Bear's five supervisory capacities, the skills a person needs to oversee an

310
00:19:52,250 --> 00:19:56,563
AI, is problem formulation: seeing which question the model is really being asked,

311
00:19:56,583 --> 00:20:00,563
and which downstream decisions its answer feeds. That doesn't respect the line

312
00:20:00,583 --> 00:20:04,230
between technical and political. Keeping the line clean means handing the

313
00:20:04,250 --> 00:20:07,563
formulation to someone else, and any delegation can be tested.

314
00:20:07,583 --> 00:20:12,282
Now the limit case. A twenty twenty-six study called Agents of Chaos red-teamed

315
00:20:12,302 --> 00:20:17,363
autonomous AI agents in a live lab. Its sixth case study, Agents Reflect Provider

316
00:20:17,383 --> 00:20:22,445
Values, sent benign but politically sensitive prompts to an agent backed by a model

317
00:20:22,465 --> 00:20:23,897
from a Chinese provider.

318
00:20:23,917 --> 00:20:28,459
The authors report that the provider's API repeatedly cut off responses with a

319
00:20:28,479 --> 00:20:32,670
generic unknown error on those topics, silently stopping the agent from completing

320
00:20:32,690 --> 00:20:37,934
valid tasks. The team that deployed the agent made none of those choices, and its

321
00:20:37,954 --> 00:20:39,688
users could not see them.

322
00:20:39,708 --> 00:20:43,958
Draw the causal graph. The bias-carrying path doesn't run through the deploying

323
00:20:43,978 --> 00:20:48,583
team's data or code. It runs through the provider's training and hosting, upstream

324
00:20:48,603 --> 00:20:52,852
of everything that engineer controls. No intervention by the deploying team can

325
00:20:52,872 --> 00:20:53,563
reach it.

326
00:20:53,583 --> 00:20:57,563
The deploying engineer's options are: switch providers, which is rarely possible at

327
00:20:57,583 --> 00:21:01,563
organizational scale; accept the bias, which is often what happens; or add

328
00:21:01,583 --> 00:21:04,897
downstream filtering and validation. The procedure still applies. Sometimes its

329
00:21:04,917 --> 00:21:09,563
answer is that the highest-leverage point is outside your reach, which is useful to

330
00:21:09,583 --> 00:21:12,897
know before you spend six months on the wrong thing.

331
00:21:12,917 --> 00:21:17,150
That's the accountability move. Assigning the owner of a bias isn't assigning blame

332
00:21:17,170 --> 00:21:21,730
to a person. It's naming whoever sits at the leverage point. Sometimes that's you,

333
00:21:21,750 --> 00:21:25,983
because you chose the sample. Sometimes the labeling team, who wrote the manual.

334
00:21:26,003 --> 00:21:30,236
Sometimes the deployment owner, who built the review room. And sometimes the model

335
00:21:30,256 --> 00:21:30,563
provider.

336
00:21:30,583 --> 00:21:35,416
The graph is a machine for un-diffusing responsibility. It points at a node, and a

337
00:21:35,436 --> 00:21:39,946
node has an owner. Bear connects this to a question Hannah Arendt asked about

338
00:21:39,966 --> 00:21:44,475
systems where harm is the output of many role-bound people, none of whom feels

339
00:21:44,495 --> 00:21:47,063
accountable: the same question, restated for a pipeline.

340
00:21:47,083 --> 00:21:52,328
Where is Bear unsure? What would change his mind: a debiasing algorithm shown to

341
00:21:52,348 --> 00:21:56,088
remove structural bias, robustly, across domains and base-rate regimes, without

342
00:21:56,108 --> 00:22:00,976
touching the deployment context. He isn't aware of such a demonstration, and says

343
00:22:00,996 --> 00:22:02,480
the literature isn't finished.

344
00:22:02,500 --> 00:22:07,342
And what he's still puzzling over: telling dataset bias from label bias when all you

345
00:22:07,362 --> 00:22:10,908
have is the labeled training set. They're causally distinct but observationally

346
00:22:10,928 --> 00:22:15,122
close. The diagnosis usually needs access to the labeling process, which other teams

347
00:22:15,142 --> 00:22:18,688
control. He says he doesn't know how to do this well.

348
00:22:19,208 --> 00:22:23,629
Let's recap with Claude. Bias is a systematic offset in an estimator, and more data

349
00:22:23,649 --> 00:22:28,070
does not remove it. Ten mechanisms put that offset in at different points of the

350
00:22:28,090 --> 00:22:31,919
pipeline. Dataset, label and structural bias each need a different fix. Two fairness

351
00:22:31,939 --> 00:22:36,063
claims can both be correct; choosing between them is a values call. Pearl's first

352
00:22:36,083 --> 00:22:39,616
two rungs separate correlating a bias from causing it. Find the highest-leverage

353
00:22:39,636 --> 00:22:41,688
path, then name whoever owns that node.

354
00:22:41,708 --> 00:22:46,562
Help me run a leverage analysis on an AI system I know. I'll describe a decision it

355
00:22:46,582 --> 00:22:50,576
makes. Ask me for the protected attribute, the training data, the labels, and who

356
00:22:50,596 --> 00:22:54,589
reviews the output. Then draw the causal graph, list every path from the protected

357
00:22:54,609 --> 00:22:58,603
attribute to the outcome, say which intervention blocks the most paths, and name who

358
00:22:58,623 --> 00:23:00,897
owns that node. Flag anything you are guessing.

359
00:23:00,917 --> 00:23:06,188
Bias: Where It Enters and Who Is Responsible. At Nik Bear Brown.
